igns, develops, markets, and sells athletic footwear, apparel, equipment, accessories, and services for men, women, and kids in North America, Europe, the Middle East, Africa, Greater China, the Asia Pacific, and Latin America. The company offers its products under the NIKE, Jordan, Converse, Jumpman, Chuck Taylor, All Star, One Star, Star Chevron, and Jack Purcell trademarks. It also provides a line of performance equipment and accessories, including bags, socks, sport balls, eyewear, timepieces, digital devices, bats, gloves, protective equipment, and other equipment for sports activities; and sports apparel, as well as sells products to wholesale customers and directly to consumers through NIKE Direct operations; distributes and licenses casual sneakers, apparel, and accessories; and markets apparel with licensed college and professional team and league logos. In addition, the company offers consumer services and experiences, including sport focused events and activations; fitness and activity apps; sport, fitness, and wellness content; and digital services and features in retail stores. It sells its products to footwear stores; sporting goods stores; athletic specialty stores; department stores; skate, tennis, and golf shops; and other wholesale accounts through NIKE-owned retail stores, independent distributors, licensees, sales representatives, and digital platforms. The company was formerly known as Blue Ribbon Sports, Inc. and changed its name to NIKE, Inc. in May 1971. NIKE, Inc. was founded in 1964 and is headquartered in Beaverton, Oregon.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Elliott J. Hill | CEO, President & Director | Age 61 | Compensation: $4.2M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 73K people. Revenue per employee of $636K ranks in the above the sector median among Consumer Cyclical peers, with revenue growing 6%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $30K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $10K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 7\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Nike, Inc. is a large-cap consumer cyclical business with a $55.4B market cap on $46.4B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -3% over the past three years and +4% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 34% against a 9% cost of capital \u2014 a 25-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat, with gross margins sitting at 45%.","The business throws off $2.2B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 5 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, with adequate 0.92\u00d7 cash conversion.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.1\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 13\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield of 4.6% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 32% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.3227119292286555},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 8.6% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are overwhelmingly selling (100% sell ratio) \u2014 the people who know the business want less of it","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","ratio":0.0},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Consumer Cyclical peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer demand is fundamentally cyclical \u2014 a recession compresses both unit volume and pricing power, and operating leverage cuts both ways.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"E-commerce margin compression and tariff exposure \u2014 digital competition erodes pricing power while trade policy reshuffles supply-chain cost structures overnight.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","38 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk","Consumer Cyclical sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 8% over 3 months"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Is Nike's (NYSE:NKE) Turnaround Finally Gaining Momentum?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/consumer/is-nikes-nysenke-turnaround-finally-gaining-momentum","date":"2026-11-26","timestamp":1795700880.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.715,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"What Could Shape Nike (NYSE:NKE) Amid Changing Market Conditions?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/bluechip/what-could-shape-nike-nysenke-amid-changing-market-conditions","date":"2026-11-25","timestamp":1795622160.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Nike (NKE) Stock May Be 14% Undervalued Despite New Product Rollouts","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://sim